{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774316478,"nonce":"K3l1VjJhivMsPhH5mFJsdMxNWHk=","title":"Pass n_replicates explicitly instead of inferring from array shapes","message":"## Summary\n\nTaskTrainer infers n_replicates by calling tree_infer_batch_size(model), which checks that all array leaves have the same first dimension. This is fragile — any leaf that doesn't carry the ensemble dimension (e.g., StateIndex.init arrays for intervenors/disturbance fields) causes a crash.\n\nCurrent workaround: exclude StateIndex nodes from the check (commit 7a90172). But this is band-aid — the real fix is to pass n_replicates explicitly through the training pipeline rather than inferring it.\n\n## Root cause\n\nWhen get_ensemble vmaps model construction, StateIndex objects are not vmapped (they're marker/reference objects). Their .init fields retain the original shape (e.g., (2,) for a 2D force vector) instead of getting the ensemble dimension (5, 2). This is correct behavior for StateIndex — the init value is shared across replicates. But tree_infer_batch_size doesn't know this.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\n1. Add n_replicates as an explicit parameter to TaskTrainer.__call__ (optional, inferred as fallback)\n2. Thread it through from the training config (hps.model.n_replicates is already available)\n3. Use tree_infer_batch_size only as a validation check (assert n_replicates == inferred), not as the primary source\n4. Long-term: consider named axes or explicit batch dimension metadata on the model\n\n## Affected code\n\n- feedbax/train.py line 280: tree_infer_batch_size(model)\n- feedbax/_tree.py: tree_infer_batch_size implementation\n- Any caller that creates ensembled models with intervenors/disturbance fields\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.6 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e","files":null}]}